/**
@defgroup unit_tests Unit tests
@brief CMocka-based unit tests for the LAME library and frontend.

The tests under @c test/unit exercise individual functions in isolation using
the [CMocka](https://cmocka.org/) framework. They are optional: the build only
compiles and runs them when configured with

@code
./configure --enable-unit-tests
@endcode

(default off). With the flag set, @c "make check" builds and runs every test
under @c test/unit and reports each one's pass/fail status. Only the pre-2.0 CMocka macro set is used, a
temporary restriction until cmocka 2.0 is more widely available across
distributions.

@section unit_tests_running Running the tests

@code
./configure --enable-unit-tests
make
make check
@endcode

The flag is what matters: the whole @c test/unit tree is wrapped in
@c "if ENABLE_UNIT_TESTS", so without it @c "make check" has nothing to build
and succeeds without running a single test. Having CMocka installed does not
enable them on its own.

@subsection unit_tests_check_vs_test "make check" is not "make test"

The two targets sound alike and answer different questions. Only one of them
can fail.

<table>
<tr><th>Target</th><th>What it does</th><th>Fails the build?</th></tr>
<tr><td>@c "make check"</td>
    <td>Builds and runs the CMocka tests.</td>
    <td>Yes &mdash; a failing test exits non-zero. This is the gate.</td></tr>
<tr><td>@c "make test"</td>
    <td>Encodes @c testcase.wav and counts the bytes that differ from the
        committed @c testcase.mp3.</td>
    <td>No &mdash; it prints a number and always succeeds.</td></tr>
</table>

@c "make test" measures bitstream drift, and its count is expected to be
non-zero on a different processor, compiler or optimization level; the number
is only meaningful to a developer comparing it against the same build before a
change. Use @c "make check" to find out whether anything is broken.

@subsection unit_tests_results Where the results are

@c "make check" prints a one-line PASS/FAIL per test and a summary. When
something fails, the detail is in the files automake leaves in the build tree:

- @c test/unit/test-suite.log &mdash; the failing tests' output, collected.
  It sits next to the tests, not at the top of the build tree.
- @c test/unit/<name>.log &mdash; one per test, including the ones that
  passed. CMocka's own message for the failing assertion, with its file and
  line, is here.
- @c test/unit/<name>.trs &mdash; the machine-readable result automake
  itself reads.

A single test can also be run directly: the binaries are built in
@c test/unit and take no arguments.

@subsection unit_tests_coverage Running them under coverage

Which lines the suite actually reaches is a separate question from whether it
passes, and has its own harness &mdash; see @ref maintainer_coverage.

@section unit_tests_seam Testing static functions

Several of the functions under test are @c static and have no external linkage.
To reach them, the corresponding test compiles the whole translation unit in
directly with @c \#include (for example @c test_parse_pathlen.c does
@c \#include @c "parse.c"). That pulls in the file's other external references,
which a small companion "stubs" object then satisfies for the linker:

- @c test_stubs.c &mdash; the frontend globals and console/file helpers that
  @c get_audio.c drags in.
- @c parse_test_stubs.c &mdash; the console/file helpers that @c parse.c drags
  in (@c parse.c defines the frontend global-config blocks itself, so those are
  not re-stubbed).

libmp3lame supplies the real @c lame_* API. Where a test needs to observe
behaviour that the return value alone cannot reveal (such as a parser spinning
rather than returning), it interposes a libc call with the linker's
@c --wrap mechanism.

@section unit_tests_list The tests

- @c test_sanity.c &mdash; harness smoke test (@ref test_sanity.c).
- @c test_get_audio_aiff.c &mdash; AIFF FORM-size underflow guard in
  @c parse_aiff_header() (@ref test_get_audio_aiff.c).
- @c test_parse_pathlen.c &mdash; positional input/output filename length bound
  in @c set_path_arg() (@ref test_parse_pathlen.c).
- @c test_merge_argv.c &mdash; argument-vector sizing and the @c merge_argv()
  count clamp (@ref test_merge_argv.c).
*/
